it is march 17 2022 and you're watching  the code report it's about time we had a  talk about time you would think it's a  pretty simple concept time began on  january 1st 1970 with the unix epic at  any moment we can get the number of  milliseconds elapsed from this date on  any computer using a method like date  now this is called incremental time but  one problem is that the earth doesn't  rotate evenly because it's actually flat  and the international earth rotation  service occasionally mandates a leap  second where an additional second is  added to the last minute of the day  things start getting weird when we talk  about time zones which are calculated  relative to greenwich mean time on the  pacific we subtract eight hours but in  newfoundland they only subtract three  and a half hours and in nepal they add  five and three quarters then daylight  savings comes around and all the time  zones change except in places like  arizona and hawaii that don't do a time  change in the south pacific they're 13  hours ahead which requires them to skip  an entire day you could celebrate new  year's on christmas island spend the  entire next day recovering from your  hangover then fly to jarvis island in  time to celebrate new year's eve again  now when it comes to the entire year the  gregorian calendar is a ridiculous  abstraction some months have 28 days  while others have 31 but every other  counting system we use like weeks and  days have consistent intervals it  doesn't have to be this way the  cotsworth plan which was presented in  1902 divides the year into 13 months  each month with four weeks or 28 days  with one rest day somewhere in the  middle it makes so much more sense and  aligns better with the lunar cycle for  developers it would make the complicated  matter of finding the duration of  multiple months a simple matter of  multiplying by 28 but there may be some  good news when it comes to keeping track  of time there's a bill going through  congress right 